{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790265033,"nonce":"Fxv8Ch+jT1Gl1mJ1NX9qR6eMGZg=","title":"Pattern syntax: extractors with a pattern on the right, binders, object patterns","message":"16 conformance rows failed to parse; about half were switch-pattern syntax that Haxe 4.3.6 accepts:\n- extractor whose right side is a pattern, chained or nested: `_.get() =\u003e {a: _.f() =\u003e s1}`, `_ * 2 =\u003e _ + 1 =\u003e 15`, `[a, _ * 10 =\u003e 20]` (Issue7367, 9905)\n- binder `name = pattern`: `case v = null`, `{x: x = \"foo\"}`, `[a = 1 | 2, b = 2 | 1]` (Issue6724, 3088, 2610)\n- object patterns with pattern fields.\n\nParser: Pattern::Extractor's value is a Pattern; new Pattern::Bind; `{...}` whose fields need pattern syntax parses as Pattern::Object (a plain literal still parses as Const).\n\nLowering: switch.rs turns array, object, binder and extractor patterns into a wildcard case guarded by a test on the subject, with bindings declared before the body (generalising the existing extractor/array guard). A nested enum constructor becomes an inner `switch` binding its arguments to fresh names, so the real matcher still does the enum test. `switch [a, b]` matches each element against its own expression (tuple matching) rather than indexing a literal whose element type is the first element's.\n\nAlso: a return inside a `switch` EXPRESSION did not type an un-annotated function (find_return_type_in_expression had no Switch arm), so `function f() { switch m { case X: return x; } }` was `-\u003e void`.\n\nPlain top-level object patterns were broken at HEAD as well: a capture in `case {s: s}` was unbound (\"Cannot find name\"), a constant field `case {s: \"abc\"}` never matched.\n\nKnown leftovers: the subject expression is re-evaluated per guard (as before); Issue11157's `_ \u003e 0 \u0026\u0026 _ \u003c 12` is rejected by Haxe 4.3.6 itself; Issue3198/9405/6531 are macro tests.","files":null}]}